import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Operate the PostgreSQL durability provider."
  description="The implemented provider targets PostgreSQL 16 and 17 with checksum-pinned migrations, secure transport defaults, bounded pools, and fail-closed recovery."
  section="PostgreSQL provider"
>
  <aside class="docs-callout" aria-labelledby="provider-boundary-title">
    <h2 id="provider-boundary-title">Pre-alpha operational boundary</h2>
    <p>
      The provider is real and heavily tested, but it is unpublished and still
      assumes a trusted server-side pool. Role-separated server procedures,
      retention, restore qualification, first-party model/tool integration, and
      cross-tenant fairness remain release blockers.
    </p>
  </aside>

  <section aria-labelledby="sequence-title">
    <h2 id="sequence-title">Migrate explicitly, then connect</h2>
    <p>
      Use separate migration and runtime credentials. Migration is a deployment
      action, not an implicit side effect of every application startup.
    </p>
    <div class="docs-code" data-code-block>
      <div class="docs-code-head">
        <span>Rust · current pre-alpha API</span>
        <button
          class="copy-button affordance"
          type="button"
          data-copy-button
          data-copy-target="postgres-connect-code"
        >
          <span data-copy-label aria-live="polite">Copy</span>
        </button>
      </div>
      <pre
        id="postgres-connect-code"
        tabindex="0"><code>use stateknot_store_postgres::&#123;PostgresStore, PostgresStoreOptions&#125;;

let options = PostgresStoreOptions::default();
PostgresStore::migrate_database(&migration_url, options.clone()).await?;
let store = PostgresStore::connect(&runtime_url, options).await?;
store.health_check().await?;</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
      Startup refuses missing, newer, older, incomplete, or checksum-mismatched
      schemas. Do not catch that refusal and continue with a partially
      compatible database.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="defaults-title">
    <h2 id="defaults-title">Secure and bounded defaults</h2>
    <dl class="docs-definition-list">
      <div>
        <dt>Transport</dt>
        <dd>
          TLS certificate and hostname verification is the default. URL query
          parameters cannot silently weaken it.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Pool</dt>
        <dd>
          1–16 connections, five-second acquire timeout, ten-minute idle
          retirement, and thirty-minute maximum connection lifetime.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Transactions</dt>
        <dd>
          Five-second lock timeout and fifteen-second statement timeout; invalid
          or unbounded timing relationships fail during connection setup.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Ownership</dt>
        <dd>
          Thirty-second run leases with a five-minute renewal horizon. Outbox
          network attempts use a fixed, non-renewable one-minute lease.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="graph-registry-title">
    <h2 id="graph-registry-title">
      Treat graph versions as immutable releases
    </h2>
    <p>
      Migration 13 adds a tenant-scoped compiled graph registry. The key is the
      exact graph owner, name, and version; canonical bytes and the definition
      digest may never change beneath that key. Register the graph before a run
      can depend on its reference.
    </p>
    <div class="docs-code" data-code-block>
      <div class="docs-code-head">
        <span>Rust · idempotent graph registration</span>
        <button
          class="copy-button affordance"
          type="button"
          data-copy-button
          data-copy-target="postgres-register-graph-code"
        >
          <span data-copy-label aria-live="polite">Copy</span>
        </button>
      </div>
      <pre
        id="postgres-register-graph-code"
        tabindex="0"><code>let outcome = store
    .register_graph_definition(tenant_id, compiled_graph)
    .await?;</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
      Identical retries converge on the original registration. Different bytes
      under the same version return a conflict. Every load recompiles and checks
      canonical bytes, redundant key columns, digest, and tenant binding;
      claimed recovery quarantines missing or corrupt pinned evidence under its
      exact live fence.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="tls-title">
    <h2 id="tls-title">Do not weaken TLS casually</h2>
    <p>
      <code>VerifyFull</code> is appropriate for production. <code
        >RequireEncryption</code
      >
      encrypts without authenticating the server and is therefore a deliberate downgrade.
      <code>Disabled</code> is only for a trusted local socket or an isolated test
      network.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="permissions-title">
    <h2 id="permissions-title">Separate database roles</h2>
    <p>
      The migration role needs the ability to create the owned schema and
      migration table. The runtime role needs only the documented table and
      projection operations; it should not receive DDL or update/delete access
      to immutable journal, checkpoint, invocation revision, attempt, result,
      graph definition, or consumption history.
    </p>
    <p>
      The exact current grant surface is maintained in the repository's
      <a
        href="https://github.com/StateKnot/StateKnot/blob/main/docs/postgresql-provider.md"
        >provider operations document</a
      >. Review it against the release you deploy rather than copying grants
      from an older article.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="qualification-title">
    <h2 id="qualification-title">Qualification and current limits</h2>
    <p>
      CI runs the mandatory integration suite independently against PostgreSQL
      16 and 17: 91 provider cases plus twelve durable Runtime scenarios per
      version. The suite covers migrations, lost acknowledgements, fencing,
      corruption rejection, concurrency, rollback injection, outbox recovery,
      durable waits, quarantine, immutable graph registration/recovery,
      ready-node planning, delayed-retry scheduling, lifecycle commits, and
      tenant-scoped Agent Loop execution.
    </p>
    <p>
      Production release still requires role isolation, retention and garbage
      collection, failover and point-in-time restore exercises, stale-race
      qualification, first-party model/tool adapters, cross-tenant fairness, and
      the stable public Agent API.
    </p>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
